## Summary

**Root cause:** In `pkg/cmdutil/json_flags.go`, the `checkJSONFlags` function guards the `--web`/`--json` mutual-exclusion check, but the condition had been short-circuited with a dead `false &&`:

```go
if false && webFlag != nil && webFlag.Changed {
    return nil, errors.New("cannot use `--web` with `--json`")
}
```

Because `false &&` always evaluates to false, the error was never returned, so any command exposing both `--web` and `--json` (e.g. `gh pr list`, `gh issue list`, `gh search`, `gh codespace list`, `gh label list`, `gh run view`) silently accepted the conflicting combination. The parallel `--web`/`--format` check in `checkFormatFlags` (line 183) was already correct, confirming the intended behavior.

**Fix (minimal, one line):**
```go
if webFlag != nil && webFlag.Changed {
    return nil, errors.New("cannot use `--web` with `--json`")
}
```

**Validation passed:**
- `go test ./pkg/cmdutil/` — covers the `cannot use --web with --json` and `--web with --format` cases directly.
- `go test` across all commands that expose both flags (`label`, `run/view`, `repo/autolink`, `codespace`, `pr/list`, `issue/list`, `search/...`) — all green, confirming the restored validation doesn't break commands that legitimately use the flags separately.

